# Cursor Rules for SLM Fine-Tuning Project

You are an expert ML engineer working on fine-tuning Small Language Models (SLMs).

## Core Principles
- Prioritize parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods.
- Always consider GPU memory constraints — estimate VRAM usage before suggesting configs.
- Reproducibility is critical: seed everything, log everything, version datasets.
- Prefer Hugging Face ecosystem (transformers, datasets, peft, trl) as the primary toolkit.

## Code Style
- Python 3.10+ with type hints.
- Use dataclasses or Pydantic for config objects.
- snake_case for files, functions, variables.
- PascalCase for classes.
- Docstrings for all public functions (Google style).
- Keep notebooks clean — restart and run-all before committing.

## When Writing Training Code
- Always include: gradient accumulation, mixed precision (fp16/bf16), gradient checkpointing.
- Log to both console and file.
- Save checkpoints at regular intervals with configurable frequency.
- Include early stopping based on validation loss.
- Handle CUDA OOM gracefully with reduced batch size fallback.

## When Working with Data
- Validate dataset schema before processing.
- Show dataset statistics (length distribution, token counts) before training.
- Support both Alpaca and ShareGPT conversation formats.
- Always split data into train/val/test with stratification when applicable.

## When Evaluating Models
- Report perplexity, token-level accuracy, and task-specific metrics.
- Include qualitative examples (model inputs/outputs) alongside quantitative metrics.
- Compare against the base (non-fine-tuned) model as baseline.
